go from here what  is the future of css interest well the  answer is  i have no clue i really don't know  i spent the last five years in this  community i've  you know we made style components in  2016 and even before that i was thinking  about css and just  i've tried all the ideas i have right  i've literally tried if all the ideas i  had and then all the ideas that  everybody else had because we kept  stealing from them right  literally no ideas in my head  but here's the thing i'm sitting here  and i'm thinking you know  say 10 20 50 years down the line  do i think we're still going to be using  style components  i hope the [ __ ] not that would be sad  because we would have stagnated  right we would have stopped innovating  at that point right that would be really  sad to me i don't want that to happen  and so really what i want to get out of  this talk is i want to motivate you  to create the future of styling react  apps but also really any kind of website  um it's  much easier than you think right and if  you have any ideas if you have any  hunches if you have if you have a  feeling like  ah this could be easier how could we  make this easier start thinking about it  start tinkering  start building prototypes right share  them with the world and talk about them  and  discuss them and maybe it turns out that  your idea  is really nice like many of the ideas  that i just talked about right  for many of these ideas when when they  were announced nobody knew whether they  were nice nobody knew whether people  would like them nobody know knew whether  they even liked them  right or most of them were prototypes  that somebody threw together in like two  days and  just thought maybe this could work right  and for some ideas it turns out they  were absolutely horrible like doing css  and just by inline styles right some  ideas just turned out to be horrible  they're not good ideas which we know in  hindsight  but then other ideas actually turn out  to be really nice like this weird ipi  that glenn and i came up for  with first style components nobody seen  anything like that before right  nobody knew that whether or not that was  nice we just thought we just  you know announced that components we  were like try it and let us know what  you think and it turns out it's actually  a really nice api to use to build apps  with  um did we know that beforehand hell no  we just tried to build a prototype  that we thought might be nice right  really that's it now  if you actually have an idea and if you  want to do this here are some tips  and these don't just apply to css and  chess libraries these really generally  apply to open source projects  and pretty much any kind of project the  first one is  to make it work make it right and then  make it fast  um i see many people when they release  open source libraries or  or products they try to make it perfect  first right they try to compete with for  example style components right they're  like oh  i have an idea for css and just library  but for anybody to use this  it has to be as perfect as style  components right  now here's the thing we've worked on  style components for whatever four years  now  four and a half years now there's been  hundreds if not thousands of  collaborators working on it right  you're never going to beat that level of  quality but it doesn't matter right what  matters is whether your idea is nice or  not  the first version we built of style  components was a horrible hack  we basically took post css we ripped out  all of the node stuff  and we shipped that in the browser which  is the most bonkers thing anybody could  ever do that is not a good idea  but we just wanted to make it work we  just wanted to see we have this hunch  that that might be a nice thing to do  is that this api the style.div api might  be nice to use  we don't actually know if that's the  case but we're also never going to find  out if we don't make it work  and try it right and so we made it work  and then we shared it and we talked  about it and we got the feedback  then we took whatever feedback we got  and we made the library better we made  it right  right we people kept asking us how do i  add global styles people kept asking us  why can i adjust why can't i adjust my  global stars dynamically  people kept asking us all these  questions and we were like uh  yeah i guess you can't i guess we got to  build something to support that  right and so the api is only as good as  it is now because we got so much  feedback and because we incorporated  that feedback into our library because  we  sort of started covering all of the edge  cases and all the stuff that people  wanted  but that didn't that didn't happen at  the very beginning right we just made it  work at first and then we made it right  and then eventually  we made it fast right once we had apis  that we thought were  well-rounded that sort of covered all of  the use cases that's when we actually  got to work on making the library as  performant as it could be  um had we done that before then we would  have wasted so much work  because we would have made apis fast  that turned out to be completely wrong  right that turned out to be really bad  ideas because we had those as well right  not everything in style components was  an instant banger it just worked out the  books right  sort of some core ideas turned out to be  really nice but some others were  complete crap  and we wouldn't have known that before  getting all that feedback and making the  apis right  and that actually ties into my second  point share early and share often  right if you don't talk about your thing  if you don't share it with people  you're never going to get feedback and  without feedback you're never going to  make your library right  now that doesn't have to be external you  can also do this if you work at a big  company  you can also do this internally and  share it with people and talk to them  about it and get the feedback  but really nothing rivals the power of  open source where you can post your code  on github and literally get  thousands hundreds or thousands of eyes  on it pretty much pretty quickly  and get all that feedback going in of  course there's gonna be bad feedback  like  oh i think this is the worst idea ever  you're an idiot right that happens  that's happened to me like literally  millions of times whenever i talk about  css and judge somebody afterwards comes  up to me and it's like  yeah that was a good talk but [ __ ]  you're an idiot right  literally all the time happens so  frequently it's it's kind of funny to be  honest  um that's gonna happen and that's fine  and i don't really care about it  um but if i don't if we didn't share  style components that early and that  often and we didn't talk about the ideas  we had  we would have never figured out whether  this was nice and we would have never  been able to make our apis right sharing  is really really important  and actually if you end up experimenting  with css and js  just send me a dm on twitter send me a  dm tell me what you built and i'm going  to share with my audience because what  do i have an audience for if not for  that  right and i'm going to get you that  feedback to make sure to figure out  whether  your ideas are good ideas or not right  because i don't know  and you don't know but all of the world  might know and then thirdly and really  importantly document  all of the things a for the start for  the first style components release  we spent maybe like 20 of our time on  actually creating the library  and then we spent another 80 documenting  every single thing you could do with it  and how it worked and why it worked and  all of that stuff  and that was really vital to its success  if we hadn't documented  all the things nobody would have used it  because i i sometimes see this stroke  about open source that people go oh  if you want to figure out how this works  just read the source code but what the  [ __ ] is that like  nobody's going to read source code right  literally nobody does that i have never  done it and i don't think many other  people do i mean i do research  sometimes but not to figure out the api  if i want to use something just tell me  how to use it tell me what it can do  tell me all the edge cases tell me  everything you can't do  just tell me right document all the  things  that's really all i have for you today  like i said thank you for having me i  hope you enjoyed this talk and i look  forward to  all of your questions
 

